[comment {-*- tcl -*- doctools manpage}]
[manpage_begin "Event log" n ""]
[copyright "2004-2014, Ashok P. Nadkarni"]
[moddesc "Event Log"]
[titledesc "Commands relating to reading and writing the Windows event log"]
[require twapi_eventlog]
[description]
[para]

The [cmd twapi_evt] package implements commands for reading and writing to
the Windows Event log.

[section Publishers]

An event [emph publisher], or [emph provider] (the Windows SDK uses the term
interchangeably) defines the structure of events it may generate, the channels
to which it writes events, and localized message strings. This structure is
described by metadata in a publisher manifest that is installed on the system.

[para]
The following commands return information
related to publishers.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_publisher_events_metadata [cmd evt_publisher_events_metadata]]]
Returns the metadata for the events defined by a publisher.
[opt_def [uri #evt_publisher_message [cmd evt_publisher_message]]]
Returns the message corresponding to a message id from the publisher's
message resource.
[opt_def [uri #evt_publisher_metadata_open [cmd evt_publisher_metadata_open]]]
Returns a handle to a publisher's metadata.
[opt_def [uri #evt_publisher_metadata_property [cmd evt_publisher_metadata_property]]]
Returns the value of a publisher metadata property.
[opt_def [uri #evt_publisher_install [cmd evt_publisher_install]]]
Installs a publisher to the local system.
[opt_def [uri #evt_publisher_uninstall [cmd evt_publisher_uninstall]]]
Uninstalls a publisher from the local system.
[opt_def [uri #evt_publishers [cmd evt_publishers]]]
Returns a list of publishers installed on the local system.
[opt_def [uri #evt_twapi_install [cmd evt_twapi_install]]]
Installs the TWAPI event publisher.
[opt_def [uri #evt_twapi_uninstall [cmd evt_twapi_uninstall]]]
Uninstalls the TWAPI event publisher.
[list_end]

An [emph event] is structured data written to event log whose fields
and their format are defined by the [emph "event metadata"].




[section "Using evt Commands"]

[para]
The [uri #evt_local_session [cmd evt_local_session]] command returns a handle to
an EVT [emph session] for the Windows Event Log service on the local system using the
current user credentials.
The [uri #evt_open_session [cmd evt_open_session]] command establishes
a session to a remote system or to the local system using different user
credentials. The command [uri #evt_is_local_session [cmd evt_is_local_session]]
can be used to distinguish between handles returned by the two commands.
When no longer needed, sessions opened using [cmd evt_open_session] should be
released using [uri #evt_close_session [cmd evt_close_session]].

[para]
EVT [emph channels] implement a event stream to which event providers can write
events. The commands [uri #evt_channels [cmd evt_channels]] and
[uri #evt_publishers [cmd evt_publishers]] return the names of the channels and
publishers on a system respectively. Channels have configuration properties.
The configuration database is opened with
[uri #evt_open_channel_config [cmd evt_open_channel_config]]. Properties
can then be retrieved and set through
[uri #evt_get_channel_config [cmd evt_get_channel_config]] and
[uri #evt_set_channel_config [cmd evt_set_channel_config]].
Changes made are saved with
[uri #evt_save_channel_config [cmd evt_save_channel_config]].
The configuration properties are shown in the table below and
mapped to the Window SDK enumeration
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_channel_config_property_id EVT_CHANNEL_CONFIG_PROPERTY_ID].

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -enabled]
[const EvtChannelConfigEnabled]
[opt_def -isolation]
[const EvtChannelConfigIsolation]
[opt_def -type]
[const EvtChannelConfigType]
[opt_def -owningpublisher]
[const EvtChannelConfigOwningPublisher]
[opt_def -classiceventlog]
[const EvtChannelConfigClassicEventlog]
[opt_def -access]
[const EvtChannelConfigAccess]
[opt_def -loggingretention]
[const EvtChannelLoggingConfigRetention]
[opt_def -loggingautobackup]
[const EvtChannelLoggingConfigAutoBackup]
[opt_def -loggingmaxsize]
[const EvtChannelLoggingConfigMaxSize]
[opt_def -logfilepath]
[const EvtChannelLoggingConfigLogFilePath]
[opt_def -publishinglevel]
[const EvtChannelPublishingConfigLevel]
[opt_def -publishingkeywords]
[const EvtChannelPublishingConfigKeywords]
[opt_def -publishingcontrolguid]
[const EvtChannelPublishingConfigControlGuid]
[opt_def -publishingbuffersize]
[const EvtChannelPublishingConfigBufferSize]
[opt_def -publishingminbuffers]
[const EvtChannelPublishingConfigMinBuffers]
[opt_def -publishingmaxbuffers]
[const EvtChannelPublishingConfigMaxBuffers]
[opt_def -publishinglatency]
[const EvtChannelPublishingConfigLatency]
[opt_def -publishingclocktype]
[const EvtChannelPublishingConfigClockType]
[opt_def -publishingsidtype]
[const EvtChannelPublishingConfigSidType]
[opt_def -publisherlist]
[const EvtChannelPublisherList]
[opt_def -publishingfilemax]
[const EvtChannelPublishingConfigFileMax]
[list_end]

[para]
Event consumers can consume events from channels in real time or from a saved
event file using the the [uri #evt_query [cmd evt_query]] command. This command
returns a handle to a result set containing matching events. These can then be
retrieved by [uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]] and further processed with
one of the rendering commands below.

[list_begin opt]

[opt_def [uri #evt_decode_event [cmd evt_decode_event]]]
Returns content of an event as a dictionary including system-defined,
publisher-defined fields and formatted messages.
[opt_def [uri #evt_decode_events [cmd evt_decode_events]]]
Returns a [uri base.html#recordarrays recordarray] containing
content of multiple events in the same form as returned by
[cmd evt_decode_event].
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_logpath [cmd evt_event_logpath]]]
Returns the channel or file from which the event was read.
[opt_def [uri #evt_render_event [cmd evt_render_event]]]
Returns content of an event as XML.
[opt_def [uri #evt_render_values [cmd evt_render_event]]]
Returns list of specific field values from an event as selected
by a render context.
[list_end]

A [emph "render context"] selects the event fields that are to
be rendered from an event. The following functions create a render context.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_system_render_context [cmd evt_system_render_context]]]
Returns a handle to a render context that includes all system-defined fields.
[opt_def [uri #evt_user_render_context [cmd evt_user_render_context]]]
Returns a handle to a render context that includes all publisher-defined fields.
[opt_def [uri #evt_xpaths_render_context [cmd evt_xpaths_render_context]]]
Returns a handle to a render context that includes fields matching
XPATH expressions.
[list_end]

[para]
An EVT [emph bookmark] identifies a specific event in an event log. Bookmarks are
created with [uri #evt_create_bookmark [cmd evt_create_bookmark]] and
set to point to a specific event either by passing a
XML representation previously generated with
[uri #evt_render_bookmark [cmd evt_render_bookmark]] to that command or
with [uri #evt_update_bookmark [cmd evt_update_bookmark]] when given
a event handle. A bookmark may be used to resume processing from its
targeted event in an event query by passing it to
[uri #evt_seek [cmd evt_seek]] or [uri #evt_subscribe [cmd evt_subscribe]].

[para]
All handles returned by the [cmd evt_*] commands, such as those returned from
[uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]] and [uri #evt_query [cmd evt_query]]
must be closed by passing them to [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]].

[para]
Event logs can be exported to file using one of the following functions.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_archive_exported_log [cmd evt_archive_exported_log]]]
Archives an exported event log file with localized strings.
[opt_def [uri #evt_export_log [cmd evt_export_log]]]
Exports a event log channel to a log file.
[list_end]

[para]
To monitor the event log in real time, use the
[uri #evt_subscribe [cmd evt_subscribe]] command.

[section "Commands"]

[para]
[emph {
    Many commands dealing with the event log require the process to be running
    with administrative privileges and/or in elevated mode.
}]


[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd eventlog_log] [arg MESSAGE] [opt [arg options]]]
Writes a record into the specified event log. This is a wrapper around
[uri \#eventlog_open [cmd eventlog_open]],
[uri \#eventlog_write [cmd eventlog_write]],
[uri \#eventlog_close [cmd eventlog_close]] for simple
logging needs and should be used for applications that do not have
a message file.
[nl]
The following options may be specified to control the values of the various
fields in the event log record:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -system] [arg SYSTEMNAME]] Specifies the system on which
the event log resides. By default, this is the local system.
[opt_def [cmd -source] [arg SOURCENAME]] Specifies a event log source. The
returned handle will refer to the event log to which events from
[arg SOURCENAME] are logged. This defaults to the filename portion (without
the path or extension) of the executable file.
[opt_def [cmd -type] [arg EVENTTYPE]] Indicates the type or severity of
the event. [arg EVENTTYPE] should be one of [const success], [const error],
[const warning], [const information] (default), [const auditsuccess] or [const auditfailure].
[opt_def [cmd -category] [arg CATEGORY]] This should be a integer corresponding
to a category id in the event source's category or message file. This value
defaults to [const 0].
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_archive_exported_log] [arg LOGPATH] [opt [arg options]]]
Archives the exported event log in file [arg LOGPATH]
along with formatting information so
that it can be viewed on systems where the event publishers are not installed.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -lcid] [arg LCID]]
The locale identifier to use for the formatting messages in the archive.
[opt_def [cmd -session] [arg HSESSION]]
Specifies the EVT session handle. Defaults to the local system.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_create_bookmark] [opt [arg XML]]]
Creates and returns a handle to an event bookmark. A bookmark identifies a
specific event in an event log and may be used to restart event processing from
that event at a later time.
If [arg XML] is specified, it must be the XML representation of a
bookmark as previously returned by
[uri #evt_render_bookmark [cmd evt_render_bookmark]].
[nl]
The returned bookmark identifies the event represented by the XML. If [arg XML]
is an empty string, the returned bookmark is not associated with any event and
may subsequently be updated using [cmd evt_update_bookmark].
[nl]
The returned handle should be released via [cmd evt_close].

[call [cmd evt_channels] [opt [arg HSESSION]]]
Returns a list of names of the EVT channels through the EVT session handle
[arg HSESSION]. Defaults to the local system.

[call [cmd evt_clear_log] [arg CHANNELNAME] [opt [arg options]]]
Clears the specified event channel of all events.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -backup] [arg BACKUPFILE]]
Specifies a backup file in which to save events before the event channel
is cleared.
[opt_def [cmd -session] [arg HSESSION]]
Specifies the EVT session handle. Defaults to the local system.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_close] [opt "[arg EVTHANDLE] [arg EVTHANDLE]..."]]
Close handles returned by various EVT commands such as
[uri #evt_query [cmd evt_query]] or [uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]].

[call [cmd evt_close_session] [arg EVTSESSIONHANDLE]]
Close a EVT session handle returned by
[uri #evt_open_session [cmd evt_open_session]].

[call [cmd evt_decode_event] [arg HEVENT] [opt [arg options]]]

Returns a Tcl dictionary containing the system-defined event properties,
publisher-defined event data, and formatted message strings.
[arg HEVENT] is a handle to an event such as an element of the list
returned from [uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]].

[nl]

The keys in the returned dictionary are the same as the fields of the record
array returned by [uri #evt_decode_events [cmd evt_decode_events]]. See the
documentation of that command for fields and options.

[nl]
Besides the options for [cmd evt_decode_events], the
following additional options may also be specified:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -hpublisher] [arg HPUBLISHER]]
A handle to the event publisher metadata. If not specified, the publisher
metadata for the event is extracted from the event itself.
[opt_def [cmd -values] [arg HVALUES]]
By default, the command will pick any values to be inserted into the
formatted event string from the event itself. If this option is specified,
the values will instead be picked from [arg HVALUES] which must
be a handle to a render values block.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_decode_events] [arg HEVENTLIST]]
Returns a [uri base.html#recordarrays "record array"] containing
decoded events. [arg HEVENTLIST] is a list of handles
to events as returned from [uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]].
[nl]
The returned record array always contains the following system fields
which are present in all events:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -activityid]]
GUID containing the activity id associated with the event.
[opt_def [cmd -channel]]
The channel to which the event was logged.
[opt_def [cmd -computer]]
The name of the system on which the event was logged.
[opt_def [cmd -eventid]]
The event id for the event's definition.
[opt_def [cmd -eventrecordid]]
The id of this event record.
[opt_def [cmd -keywordmask]]
An integer mask with bits corresponding to the event's keywords.
[opt_def [cmd -level]]
The numeric level for the event.
[opt_def [cmd -opcode]]
The numeric opcode for the event.
[opt_def [cmd -pid]]
The process id of the process that logged the event.
[opt_def [cmd -providerguid]]
The GUID of the logging event provider.
[opt_def [cmd -providername]]
The name of the logging event provider.
[opt_def [cmd -qualifiers]]
[opt_def [cmd -relatedactivityid]]
GUID containing the relative activity id associated with the event.
[opt_def [cmd -task]]
The numeric task for the event.
[opt_def [cmd -tid]]
The thread id of the logging thread.
[opt_def [cmd -timecreated]]
The time the event was logged in 100ns units since January 1, 1601 UTC.
[opt_def [cmd -sid]]
The SID of the account under which the logging thread was running.
[opt_def [cmd -version]]
The version of the event definition.
[list_end]

In addition, the returned record array will include the following keys
if they are specified as options to the command:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -keywords]]
Contains a list of keywords with which the event is tagged.
[opt_def [cmd -levelname]]
Contains the string associated by the publisher with the numeric level.
[opt_def [cmd -message]]
Contains the formatted message corresponding to the event.
[opt_def [cmd -opcodename]]
Contains the string associated by the publisher with the numeric opcode field.
[opt_def [cmd -taskname]]
Contains the string associated by the publisher with the numeric task field.
[opt_def [cmd -xml]]
Contains an XML fragment describing the event.
[list_end]

[nl]
The following additional options may also be specified with the command:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -ignorestring] [arg STRING]]
If specified, [arg STRING] is returned for any field that cannot be obtained.
Without this option, errors retrieving fields will generate a Tcl exception.
[opt_def [cmd -lcid] [arg LCID]]
Specifies the language in which the message is to be formatted. Defaults
to the locale of the calling thread. This option is ignored if the
[cmd -hpublisher] option is specified.
[opt_def [cmd -logfile] [arg PATH]]
Specifies the path to the logfile containing archived publisher metadata
for the event.
This option is ignored if the [cmd -hpublisher] option is specified.
[opt_def [cmd -session] [arg HSESSION]]
Specifies the EVT session handle. Defaults to the local system.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_event_logpath] [arg HEVT]]
Returns the path of the channel or file from which the event
indentified by [arg HEVT] was retrieved.

[call [cmd evt_export_log] [arg OUTFILE] [opt [arg options]]]
Exports events, optionally matching a query,
from a channel or a previously exported log file to a target log file.
[nl]
The following options may be specified to select which events are
included in the result set.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -channel] [arg CHANNELNAME]]
Specifies the event channel from which events are to be read. Cannot
be used with the [cmd -file] option.
[opt_def [cmd -file] [arg PATH]]
Specifies the event file from which events are to be read. Cannot
be used with the [cmd -channel] option.
[opt_def [cmd -ignorequeryerrors]]
Normally, if retrieving a specific event results in an error, Windows
will fail the query and a Tcl exception will be raised.
If [cmd -ignorequeryerrors] is specified, such events are simply skipped
and any further events are returned.
[opt_def [cmd -query] [arg QUERYSTRING]]
Specifies the selection criteria for events that should be included in the
returned result set.
[arg QUERYSTRING] should be [const *] (for all events, default),
an XPATH expression or a structured XML query. See
the Windows SDK for details.
[opt_def [cmd -session] [arg HSESSION]]
Specifies the EVT session handle. Defaults to the local system.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_get_channel_config] [arg HCHANCFG] [arg PROPERTY]]

Returns the value of a configuration property [arg PROPERTY] for the channel
configuration specified by the handle [arg HCHANCFG].

[call [cmd evt_is_local_session] [arg HSESSION]]
Returns `1` if the passed EVT session handle was created with
[uri #evt_local_session [cmd evt_local_session]] and `0` otherwise.

[call [cmd evt_local_session]]
Returns an EVT session handle to the local system using the current user
credentials. The handle need not be closed.

[call [cmd evt_next] [arg HEVTRESULTSET] [opt [arg options]]]
Returns a list containing events from a event query result set.
[arg HEVTRESULTSET] is a handle to a result set as returned by
[uri #evt_query [cmd evt_query]]. The returned list elements
are event handles which can be passed to
[uri #evt_decode_event [cmd evt_decode_event]]. These event handles
must be closed via [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]].
[nl]
The command returns an empty list if no more events are available.
The behaviour of further calls depends on whether [arg HEVTRESULTSET]
was returned from [uri #evt_query [cmd evt_query]] or
[uri #evt_subscribe [cmd evt_subscribe]]. In the former case, an
empty list will continue to be returned even if additional events
are logged to a channel. In the latter case, the command will return
those events.
[nl]
The cursor in the event result set is moved past the returned events
so each [cmd evt_next] returns events from the result set in sequence.
[nl]
The following options can be specified:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -count] [arg COUNT]]
Returns [arg COUNT] events from the result set. The actual number of
events returned may be less if the end of the result set is reached.
If unspecified, the number of returned events depends on various
internal tuning factors and applications must not rely on a specific
number being returned.
[opt_def [cmd -status] [arg VARNAME]]
The name of a variable in which to return status. If there is an
error when reading the next event, the command will normally generate
an Tcl exception. If this option is specified, the command will instead
return an empty list and store the error code in the specified variable.
[opt_def [cmd -timeout] [arg MILLISECONDS]]
Specifies the number of milliseconds to wait for events. A value of
[const -1] (default) indicates call should not time out.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_open_channel_config] [arg CHANNELNAME] [opt "[cmd -session] [arg HSESSION]"]]

Returns a handle to the configuration of the named channel. Opens the
configuration for an event log channel and returns a handle to it.
[cmd -session] specifies the handle for the EVT session for the channel's
host which defaults to the local system. The returned handle may be used with
[uri #evt_get_channel_config [cmd evt_get_channel_config]] and
[uri #evt_set_channel_config [cmd evt_set_channel_config]]
to query and modify the channel configuration. When longer needed,
it should be released with [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]].

[call [cmd evt_open_session] [arg SERVER] [opt [arg options]]]

Returns a EVT session handle for accessing the Windows event log service on the
local or a remote computer. [arg SERVER] specifies the name of the computer to
connect to.
[nl]
The handle may be passed to other commands that
operate on event logs on the target computer. When no longer needed, the handle
should be released using [cmd evt_close_session].
[nl]
The following options can be specified:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -user [arg USERNAME]]
Specifies the user account to use when connecting to the remote
computer. If omitted, the credentials of the current process are used.

[opt_def -domain [arg DOMAIN]]
Specifies the domain for the account specified by the [cmd -user]
option. This may be a Windows domain name or the name of the remote
computer for local accounts.

[opt_def -password [arg PASSWORD]]
Specifies the password corresponding to the account specified by the
[cmd -user] option. Must be in
[uri base.html#passwordprotection "encrypted form"].

[opt_def -authtype [arg AUTHTYPE]]
Specifies the authentication mechanism to use when connecting to the
remote computer.

[list_end]

[arg AUTHTYPE] may be take one of the following values.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [const default]]
Uses the default authentication mechanism. This is the default.
[opt_def [const negotiate]]
Uses the Negotiate security package.
[opt_def [const kerberos]]
Uses Kerberos authentication.
[opt_def [const ntlm]]
Uses NTLM authentication.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_publisher_events_metadata] [arg HPUB] [arg PROPNAMES]]

Returns a list containing the metadata for each event defined by the
publisher identified by the handle [arg HPUB]. Each event's metadata
includes the event's metadata properties as a dictionary keyed by
the event property names passed in [arg PROPNAMES]. These correspond
to the [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_event_metadata_property_id EVT_EVENT_METADATA_PROPERTY_ID]
values shown in the table below.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -channel]
EventMetadataEventChannel
[opt_def -id]
EventMetadataEventID
[opt_def -keyword]
EventMetadataEventKeyword
[opt_def -level]
EventMetadataEventLevel
[opt_def -messageid]
EventMetadataEventMessageID
[opt_def -opcode]
EventMetadataEventOpcode
[opt_def -task]
EventMetadataEventTask
[opt_def -template]
EventMetadataEventTemplate
[opt_def -version]
EventMetadataEventVersion

[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_publisher_install] [arg MANPATH] [arg RESPATH] [arg MSGPATH]]

Registers an event publisher with the local system. [arg MANPATH], [arg RESPATH]
and [arg MSGPATH] are the paths to the publisher's manifest file, resource file
and message files respectively. The command may display an elevation prompt if
the process is not running in elevated mode.


[call [cmd evt_publisher_message] [arg HPUB] [arg MESSAGEID]]
Returns the message corresponding to the message identifier
[arg MESSAGEID] using the message resources associated with the publisher
metadata specified by handle [arg HPUB].


[call [cmd evt_publisher_metadata_open] [arg PROVIDER] [opt [arg options]]]

Returns a handle to the metadata for the event provider named [arg PROVIDER].
The returned handle should be released using [cmd evt_close].
The following options may be passed to the command.

[list_begin opt]

[opt_def "[cmd -session] [arg HSESSION]"]
Specifies the EVT session handle to the system where the provider resides. Defaults to the local system.

[opt_def "[cmd -logfile] [arg PATH]"]
Path to an exported event log or archived log holding the publisher
metadata instead of the default metadata installed on the system.

[opt_def "[cmd -lcid] [arg LCID]"]
The LCID for accessing localized metadata. The default value of `0` uses
the thread's locale.

[list_end]


[call [cmd evt_publisher_metadata_property] [arg HPUB] [arg PROPNAME]]

Returns the value of a metadata property named [arg PROPNAME] for the publisher
identified by its handle [arg HPUB]. Valid [arg PROPNAME] values are shown
in the table below.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -publisherguid]
The publisher's GUID.
[opt_def -resourcefilepath]
The path to the publisher's resource file.
[opt_def -parameterfilepath]
The path to the publisher's parameter file.
[opt_def -messagefilepath]
The path to the publisher's message file.
[opt_def -helplink]
The URL of the help page.
[opt_def -publishermessageid]
The message id for retrieving the publisher's name.
[opt_def -channelreferences]
List of dictionaries of channels owned by the publisher (see below).
[opt_def -levels]
List of dictionaries of levels defined by the publisher (see below).
[opt_def -tasks]
List of dictionaries of tasks defined by the publisher (see below).
[opt_def -opcodes]
List of dictionaries of opcodes defined by the publisher (see below).
[opt_def -keywords]
List of dictionaries of keywords defined by the publisher (see below).
[list_end]

Each element of the list returned for property [cmd -channelreferences] is a
dictionary with the keys listed in the table below along with the equivalent
Windows SDK enumeration.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -channelreferenceflags]
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataChannelReferenceFlags]
- if 1, the channel was imported from another publisher's manifest.
[opt_def -channelreferenceid]
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataChannelReferenceID]
- numeric id assigned to the channel as per its manifest's `value` attribute.
[opt_def -channelreferenceindex]
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataChannelReferenceIndex]
- the provider assigned channel identifier.
[opt_def -channelreferencemessageid]
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataChannelReferenceMessageID]
- the message id for the channel localized name.

[opt_def -channelreferencepath]
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataChannelReferencePath] - the canonical name of the channel.
[list_end]

Each element of the list returned for property [cmd -levels] is a
