[call [cmd evt_publisher_uninstall] [arg MANPATH]]

Unregisters an event publisher with the local system. [arg MANPATH] is the path
to the publisher's manifest file. The command may display an elevation prompt if
the process is not running in elevated mode.

[call [cmd evt_publishers]]
Returns a list of names of the publishers registered on the system.

[call [cmd evt_query] [opt [arg options]]]
Returns a handle to a result set containing events matching the
specified criteria. Events from the result set are read using
the [uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]] command. Note that once
the end of the result set returned by [cmd evt_query] is reached,
further events that are logged will not be returned.
[nl]
After processing events, the handle must be closed
using the [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]] command.
[nl]
The following options may be specified to select which events are
included in the result set.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -channel] [arg CHANNELNAME]]
Specifies the event channel from which events are to be read. Cannot
be used with the [cmd -file] option.
[opt_def [cmd -direction] [arg DIRECTION]]
If [arg DIRECTION] is [const forward] events are returned oldest
first. If [arg DIRECTION] is [const backward], events are returned
newest first.
[opt_def [cmd -file] [arg PATH]]
Specifies the event file from which events are to be read. Cannot
be used with the [cmd -channel] option.
[opt_def [cmd -ignorequeryerrors]]
Normally, if retrieving a specific event results in an error, Windows
will fail the query and a Tcl exception will be raised.
If [cmd -ignorequeryerrors] is specified, such events are simply skipped
and any further events are returned.
[opt_def [cmd -query] [arg QUERYSTRING]]
Specifies the selection criteria for events that should be included in the
returned result set.
[arg QUERYSTRING] should be [const *] (for all events, default),
an XPATH expression or a structured XML query. See
the Windows SDK for details.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_render_bookmark] [arg HBOOOKMARK]]
Returns the XML form of a bookmark. This can then be passed to the
[uri #evt_create_bookmark [cmd evt_create_bookmark]] command to
recreate the bookmark to an event at some later time.

[call [cmd evt_render_event] [arg HEVENT]]

Returns the XML representation of the event referenced by [arg HEVENT],
including including system-defined metadata and provider-defined event data.
Localized message text is not included but can be retrieved with
[uri #evt_format_message [cmd evt_format_message]].

[call [cmd evt_render_values] [arg HEVENT] [opt [arg HCTX]]]
Returns a list of field values from event referenced by [arg HEVENT]
as per the rendering context referenced by [arg HCTX] which must be
a handle returned by
[uri #evt_xpaths_render_context [cmd evt_xpaths_render_context]],
[uri #evt_user_render_context [cmd evt_user_render_context]], or
[uri #evt_system_render_context [cmd evt_system_render_context]].
The order of values in the first two cases is the order of the XPATHS
in the render context and order of event's user data fields respectively. In
the third case, the order is always that of the
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_system_property_id "EVT_SYSTEM_PROPERTY_ID"]
enumeration in the Windows SDK.
This order can also be retrieved with the
[uri #evt_system_properties [cmd evt_system_properties]] command.

[call [cmd evt_save_channel_config] [arg HCHANCFG]]
Saves any changes made to the channel configuration.

[call [cmd evt_set_channel_config] [arg HCHANCFG] [arg PROPERTY] [arg VALUE]]

Sets the value of a configuration property [arg PROPERTY] for the channel
configuration specified by the handle [arg HCHANCFG] to [arg VALUE].
The modification does not take effect until the configuration is saved
with [uri #evt_save_channel_config [cmd evt_save_channel_config]].

[call [cmd evt_subscribe] [arg CHANNELPATH] [opt [arg options]]]
Registers for notifications when events are logged to the event
channel [arg CHANNELPATH]. Returns a pair of two handles, the first
is a handle that can be passed to [uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]]
to retrieve logged events. The second is a handle that is signalled
when events are available to be read on the channel. It can be
used in either synchronous or asynchronous mode with
[uri base.html#wait_on_handle [cmd wait_on_handle]].
[nl]
Note that Windows will not signal the handle unless a call to
[uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]] fails [emph and] a new event arrives.
The mere presence of unread events is not sufficient.
[nl]
When notifications are no longer desired, the two handles in the
returned pair must be released via [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]]
and [uri handles.html#close_handle [cmd close_handle]] respectively.
[nl]
The following options may be specified:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -ignorequeryerrors]]
Normally, if retrieving a specific event results in an error, Windows
will fail the query and a Tcl exception will be raised.
If this option is specified, such events are simply skipped
and any further events are returned.
[opt_def [cmd -includeexisting]]
By default the command will only subscribe to new events. If this option
is specified, notification is sent for existing events as well.
[opt_def [cmd -query] [arg QUERYSTRING]]
Specifies the selection criteria for events that should be included in the
returned result set.
[arg QUERYSTRING] should be [const *] (for all events, default),
an XPATH expression or a structured XML query. See
the Windows SDK for details.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_system_render_context]]
Returns a handle to a render context that can be passed to
[uri #evt_render_values [cmd evt_render_values]] to render the
system-defined properties of an event.
[nl]
The returned handle should be released using [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]]
when no longer needed.

[call [cmd evt_twapi_install]]

Registers the TWAPI event publisher with the local system. The command may
display an elevation prompt if the process is not running in elevated mode.

[call [cmd evt_twapi_uninstall]]

Unregisters the TWAPI event publisher from the local system.

[call [cmd evt_update_bookmark] [arg HBOOKMARK] [arg HEVENT]]

Updates the bookmark specified by [arg HBOOKMARK] to identify the event
specified by [arg HEVENT]. [arg HBOOKMARK] and [arg HEVENT] must be
bookmark and event handles returned by
[uri #evt_create_bookmark [cmd evt_create_bookmark]]
and [uri #evt_next [cmd uri_next]] respectively.
The event handle must refer to an event returned by [cmd evt_next]. The
bookmark may subsequently be used with commands like
[uri #evt_seek [cmd evt_seek]] or [uri #evt_query [cmd evt_query]]
to resume processing from the bookmarked event.

[call [cmd evt_user_render_context]]
Returns a handle to a render context that can be passed to
[uri #evt_render_values [cmd evt_render_values]] to render the
user-defined properties of an event under the [const UserData]
section of the event, and the [const EventData] section if no
[const UserData] section is present.
[nl]
The returned values correspond to the properties defined by the
provider in the event schema and returned in the same order.
[nl]
The returned handle should be released using [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]]
when no longer needed.

[call [cmd evt_xpaths_render_context] [arg XPATHLIST]]

Returns a handle to a render context for rendering the event properties
selected by the list of XPATH expressions passed in [arg XPATHLIST]
which must contain at least one XPATH expression.
The handle can then be passed to
[uri #evt_render_values [cmd evt_render_values]] to render the selected
property values.
[nl]
Each element of [arg XPATHLIST] specifies an XPath expression that
selects a single property from an event. The values are returned in the
same order as the corresponding XPath expressions in the list.
[nl]
The returned should be released using [cmd evt_close].

[list_end]

